    "lawCode": "Add §79-r, Civ Rts L",
    "lawSection": "Civil Rights Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S4466A\n\nSPONSOR: MAYER\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the civil rights law, in relation to the display of\nreligious items on dwellings\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nProhibits property owners or controlling entities from enforcing or\nadopting a restrictive covenant or other restriction that prohibits a\nreligious item from being displayed or affixed on any entry door or\ndoorframe.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 adds a new section 79-r to the Civil Rights Law prohibiting a\nproperty owner or controlling entity from enforcing or adopting any\nrestrictive covenant, rule, or other limitation that prohibits the\ndisplay of one or more religious items on a resident's entry door or\nentry doorframe.\n\n*Authorizes restrictions on such displays where necessary to protect\npublic health or safety, ensure compliance with federal, state, or local\nlaw, prohibit obscene or illegal displays, and to limit to reasonable\nsize.\n\n*Provides that where maintenance, repair, replacement, or other work\naffects the location of a protected display, a resident may be required\nto temporarily remove or relocate the item for the duration of such\nwork, and may restore the display upon completion, with reasonable\nnotice provided where practicable.\n\n*Defines key terms, including \"dwelling,\" \"owner,\" \"controlling entity,\"\n\"resident,\" and \"religious item,\" to apply across rental housing, condo-\nminiums, homeowners' associations, and cooperative housing arrangements.\n\nSection 2 sets the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThis legislation aims to protect the rights of an individual to display\na religious item on their door or doorframe. Many faiths require or\nencourage practicing members to display or affix items to their door or\nentry. According to the Jewish faith, a mezuzah must be placed on the\nentrances of a Jewish residence (Deuteronomy 6:9 and Deuteronomy 11:20).\nA mezuzah is a small parchment scroll containing two passages of reli-\ngious text, enclosed in a small case affixed to the doorframe.  Practic-\ning Hindus may adorn their doorways with a toran, colorful hangings\nwhich bless their home and welcome guests.1\n\nCurrently, Connecticut, Florida, Rhode Island, Illinois and Texas2\nprohibit restrictions on the placement of religious objects, including\nmezuzahs, on outer doorposts or doors.\n\nAlthough there are protections in federal law against religious discrim-\nination in housing, federal courts have determined these protections do\nnot extend to the right to place a mezuzah, toran or other religious\nitem on a doorway, if hallway items are banned for all residences\nregardless of their religious beliefs (see Bloch v.  Frischholz, 533\nF.3d 562 (7th Cir. 2008), aff'd. in part, rev'd. in part, 587 F.3d 771\n(7th Cir. 2009, en Banc)).\n\nThis legislation seeks to protect the religious rights of Jewish people,\npracticing Hindus, and members of other religions to attach a religious\nitem on their doorframe or entry door with appropriate restrictions. It\nwill prevent homeowners associations or other entities from prohibiting\nindividuals from displaying religious items on their door or doorframe.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nS8834 (2024/Mayer): Passed Senate\n\nS3674 (2022/Kaplan): died on 3rd reading\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone to the State.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the thirtieth day after it shall have\nbecome a law.\n1 https://www.hinduamerican.org/blog/whats-the-meaning-behind- hindu-\ndoor-decorations\n2 https://warwickonline.com/stories/new-law-protects-right-to- hang-re-\nligious-artifacts-in-doorways,105703\n",
